objc2 on powerpc-apple-darwin (Mac OS X 10.4 to 10.6, legacy ObjC runtime).

- Message sending: there was no MsgSendFn impl for powerpc ("Failed to find
  impl with MSG_SEND_SUPER"). Darwin ppc32 returns every struct and union
  through a hidden pointer, whatever its size, so those go through
  objc_msgSend_stret / objc_msgSendSuper_stret and everything else through
  objc_msgSend (which also returns floating point in f1).
- libobjc on 10.6 and earlier has none of the ARC entry points (objc_retain,
  objc_release, objc_autorelease*, objc_autoreleasePoolPush/Pop,
  objc_storeStrong, objc_retainBlock, the weak functions) and no
  objc_copyClassList; they arrived in 10.7. On powerpc, ffi/legacy.rs
  replaces ffi/rc.rs and implements them with -retain/-release/-autorelease,
  NSAutoreleasePool, _Block_copy and objc_getClassList. There are no zeroing
  weak references either, so objc2::rc::Weak holds a strong reference on
  powerpc: no dangling, but a cycle it was meant to break leaks.
- Like 32-bit Intel macOS, which also uses the legacy runtime: allocate with
  +alloc (objc_alloc is 10.9+), and drain autorelease pools only on the
  normal exit, not while unwinding.
- available!(): without MACOSX_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET, assume 10.4 instead of
  10.12, so version checks happen at run time.

ProtocolBuilder (objc_allocateProtocol & co., 10.7+) is left alone: it fails
at link time if a program uses it. powerpc64 isn't covered.

